## Reading a clip

Each clip is drawn as one rounded block with a thin border, so you can see
where one ends and the next begins.

- **Colour by kind**: video slate, audio green, image or still violet, text
  amber, shape teal (graphics use the same colour), nest grey, adjustment
  rose.
- **Name strip** along the top: a symbol for the kind, then the clip's name.
  A clip with no name of its own shows what it holds: its file, its text, its
  dashed accent ring, because edits to one move both (see
  [Audio](audio.md#what-stays-in-sync)).
- **Dimmed**: a clip on a hidden track, or one with both its picture and its
  sound switched off.
- **No filmstrip**: text, shape, graphic, nest and adjustment clips have no
  thumbnails. An adjustment clip is a plain rose block with only its name
  strip and action lane, and its track is sized as a picture track.

Filmstrips and waveforms come from a cache made for each media file after
over time, play it backwards, and hold one of its frames still. A constant
speed change moves the clip's end. A speed ramp leaves the clip where it is
and changes how much of the source it plays.

All of these work on **media clips** only, meaning clips of a video or audio
file. A still image, a freeze frame, text, a shape, a graphic, an adjustment
clip and a nest have no source to speed up, so the speed commands are dimmed
for them or leave them out. The one exception is
[Retime a range](#retime-a-range), which speeds up or slows down a stretch of
the whole timeline and stretches or squeezes the titles, shapes and stills in

    /// what is inside it — so that arm only answers for a nest clip whose
    /// nest id is dangling, which `validate` refuses and which draws
    /// nothing rather than a slate: the slate says "this file is missing",
    /// and no file is involved.
    fn draws(&self, clip: &Clip) -> bool {
        match &clip.source {
            Source::Media { media, .. } | Source::Still { media, .. } => {
                self.media(*media).is_none_or(|m| m.has_video)
            }
            // An adjustment clip draws by grading what is behind it
            // (spec §11 GR-6), so it is a drawing instance like a shape.
            Source::Image { .. }
            | Source::Text
            | Source::Shape
            | Source::Graphic
            | Source::Adjustment => true,
            Source::Nest { .. } => false,
        }
    }
}
